From u9ac59323a4312d0d at groups.google.com  Thu Apr 10 03:34:48 2014
From: u9ac59323a4312d0d at groups.google.com (Claire Olson)
Date: Thu, 10 Apr 2014 03:34:48 +0000
Subject: methylkit question
Message-ID: <3aqJGJT1Dl4.eLumPSaVLZ4J@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/methylkit_discussion/c/3aqJGJT1Dl4/m/eLumPSaVLZ4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

My name is Claire and I am a current graduate student at the University of Washington.  I am comparing 6 samples using whole-genome bisulfite sequencing using the R package methylKit and have a few questions I'm hoping to get answered.

First, I am wondering if I can compare these samples without designating a single sample as a control.  And if this isn't possible how this is going to skew my results by assigning a sample to be a control when in reality it isn't?  I am comparing DNA methylation patterns throughout various developmental stages so I therefore do not have a control sample in my dataset.

Also, I am wondering how exactly the differentially methylated regions (DMRs) are calculated.  I am assuming that the coverage is taken into account for the calculation of DMRs, and am a bit confused by my results.  Specifically, I am only obtaining 15 DMRs between two samples (out of ~112,000 loci), however my correlation coefficient is only 0.82 between these two samples.  Any input you can give me would be greatly appreciated and I thank you for your time.
--
Claire E. Olson

University of Washington
School of Aquatic and Fishery Sciences
che...@u.washington.edu
students.washington.edu/che625 <http://students.washington.edu/che625>

From u38c56d0e82a34280 at groups.google.com  Thu Apr 10 06:13:00 2014
From: u38c56d0e82a34280 at groups.google.com (Dhruva Chandramohan)
Date: Thu, 10 Apr 2014 06:13:00 +0000
Subject: Re: [methylkit_discussion] methylkit question
Message-ID: <3aqJGJT1Dl4.6x3akupqADAJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <3aqJGJT1Dl4.eLumPSaVLZ4J@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
References: <3aqJGJT1Dl4.eLumPSaVLZ4J@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/methylkit_discussion/c/3aqJGJT1Dl4/m/6x3akupqADAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Just to clarify- are you doing an n-of-1 study, with the data drawn at different time points? If so, I would suggest you run the analysis multiple times, with a different time point as the 'case' (treatment=1) each time (you can use the reorganize fuction in methylKit to change your treatment vector to do this). This will allow you to identify methylation differences that are characteristic for each time point. See how that compares to similar array-based studies as appropriate for your tissue and species: see this paper <http://genomebiology.com/2013/14/10/r115> and some of its references.

Two different tests are used depending on the number of samples: if you're comparing 1 vs 1, Fisher's exact test <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher's_exact_test> is used. Otherwise, logistic regression <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logistic_regression> (also known as a binomial GLM <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_linear_model> with a logit link) is used to compare the means of the two groups. In either case, coverage is taken into account. If you're using the get.methylDiff function, it's filtering for sites with qvalues<.01 and methylation differences of >25%. Depending on your data, this may be too stringent- you have a relatively low number of sites, so I'd consider raising the qvalue threshold.

Dhruva

